# linux_binder_idl version - the Binder SDK / AIDL toolchain revision this
# rdk-halif-aidl snapshot is built against.
#
# build_binder.sh checks out this git ref (commit, tag or branch) when it
# clones the toolchain. Keep it pinned so the committed module-local
# generated C++ always matches the generator that produced it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TESTING PIN - NOT A RELEASE.
#
# Pointed at the linux_binder_idl branch carrying the current build system
# (rdkcentral/linux_binder_idl PR #69): protocol 8 by default, BINDER_PROTOCOL
# and TARGET_BITNESS in place of switches that read backwards, and the QEMU
# kernel matrix that proves them.
#
# It is a branch on purpose: the switch names this repository now passes do not
# exist in 2.6.0, and CMake IGNORES an unknown -D and succeeds - so building
# against the old tag would silently produce a protocol-7 SDK with a green
# result. Testing against the branch is what makes the new switches real rather
# than ignored.
#
# This MUST return to a released tag before merge. A branch moves underneath
# you, so the committed module-local C++ stops being reproducible from the pin,
# which is the whole reason this file exists.
#
#   revert to:  2.7.0        once it is cut and carries PR #69
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 2.6.0 (the previous pin) shipped the AIDL surface dump & structural diff
# toolchain (linux_binder_idl#27/#48): aidl_ops dump-surface emits the declared
# surface as canonical deterministic text; diff-surface classifies the
# structural difference (breaking / major / none) - the engine consumed by the
# release audit gate (#633/#568). Its AIDL generator is byte-identical to
# 2.5.0, so committed module-local C++ is unchanged across these bumps.
#
# Override for a one-off build with:  BINDER_VERSION=<ref> ./build_binder.sh
feature/68-build-host-aidl-default-off
